# One-pass hashed text classification pilot v1

## Hypothesis

A stateless hashed representation plus a one-pass bounded-state linear
classifier can recover most of the full sparse Ridge quality while reducing
method wall time.  It is locally competitive only if macro-F1 loss is at most
0.05 and median method wall is at most 75% of the repeated full sparse
reference.

If it is fast but loses more than 0.05 macro-F1, the measured gap may justify a
later online document-frequency / IDF statistic study.  This pilot itself does
not test a new streaming-statistics method.

## Fixed protocol

- Same native train/test membership and metadata removal as prior experiments.
- Training order is a fixed random permutation of the native training members,
  with seeds 19, 71, and 211.  This is an order-robustness check, not a claim to
  reconstruct chronological arrival order.
- `HashingVectorizer(n_features=2**18, alternate_sign=False, norm="l2",
  stop_words="english")`; the mapping is stateless and test-independent.
- `SGDClassifier(loss="log_loss", alpha=1e-4, penalty="l2",
  learning_rate="optimal", average=True, random_state=seed, tol=None)`.
- Exactly one `partial_fit` pass in batches of 256; all 20 classes are declared
  on the first batch.  No validation or test-based tuning.
- Hash and update one training batch at a time.  Transform the complete native
  test set once after training.

## Metrics and caveats

- Primary macro-F1, secondary accuracy.
- Count permutation, all batch transforms/updates, test transform, and
  prediction in method wall.
- Record classifier coefficient/intercept bytes as the explicit learned state,
  hashed dimension, batch count, per-seed metrics/times, and cumulative peak
  RSS.
- The scikit-learn loader materializes raw text before the algorithmic stream;
  process RSS therefore bounds this implementation but does not demonstrate a
  streaming input loader.  Claims are limited to bounded feature/model state.

## Decision

Use the median across the three fixed orders.  The baseline passes only if
median macro-F1 loss is at most 0.05 and median wall ratio at most 0.75.  A
failure closes this exact hash/SGD endpoint but may motivate one prospectively
specified online-IDF comparison if speed is favorable and the quality gap is
large enough to measure.  Do not revive batch SVD or batch sampling.

